{
  "rationale": "Each test corresponds to one intent in the plan, implementing it strictly where possible, weakening when needed to avoid unstated assumptions. Tests 1–3 verify output format and basic bounds from the specification. Test 4 brute‑forces small inputs (≤8 spells) to compute the exact answer; this is justified because the specification defines correctness and the input size allows exhaustive enumeration. Tests 5–10 check necessary conditions derived from the geometry of spells: existence of a path, handling of edge cases like no horizontal spells, no full‑row spans, blocked start, and consistency of zero answers. All conditions are directly implied by the specification’s description of movement and blocking, without assuming any algorithmic details of the submission. If a test had to be dropped due to ambiguity, test 7 (no vertical, no full‑row) might be the first because it assumes a column exists avoiding all spells, which is true only if the horizontal spells do not collectively cover all columns—a subtle property not explicitly guaranteed by the spec, but we weaken it by checking only the case with no full‑row spells, which ensures a gap exists (since the board width is huge and spells are finite).",
  "tests": [
    {
      "name": "test_int",
      "source": "def test_int(run, x):
    out = run(x).strip()
    try:
        int(out)
    except ValueError:
        assert False, 'output must be an integer'"
    },
    {
      "name": "test_nonneg",
      "source": "def test_nonneg(run, x):
    out = run(x).strip()
