{
  "rationale": "We include tests that compute the exact answer via pair simulation (valid for all n) and permutation brute force (for small n) to catch any deviation. Sanity checks enforce the required range [2, n] and output format. Special cases (n=2, constant arrays) target common errors. A lower bound test ensures the answer is at least the length of any obvious Fibonacci triple. Each test is derived directly from the specification and holds for any correct solution.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_exact",
      "intent": "Compute the exact maximum length by trying all possible starting pairs (allowing same value if count ≥ 2). For each pair, simulate the Fibonacci recurrence, decrementing counts from a copy of the frequency map. Record the length when the next number is unavailable. The maximum over all pairs is the correct answer. Assert the output equals this maximum."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_bounds",
      "intent": "Parse the output as an integer. Assert it is between 2 and n inclusive. Also ensure the output string consists only of digits and an optional trailing newline, with no other characters."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_n2",
      "intent": "If n == 2, assert the output is exactly 2, since any two numbers form a valid Fibonacci‑ish prefix of length 2."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_constant",
      "intent": "If all input numbers are equal to a constant c, assert the output is n if c == 0 else 2. This follows from the recurrence: for zero, the sequence can continue indefinitely; for non‑zero, only length 2 is possible."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_lower_bound_triple",
      "intent": "Check if the multiset contains a Fibonacci triple: numbers a, b (possibly equal) such that a+b is also present, with sufficient multiplicities (for distinct a,b: at least one each of a, b, and a+b; for a=b: at least two a and one 2a). If such a triple exists, assert the output is at least 3."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_upper_bound_n",
      "intent": "Assert the output integer is ≤ n, as the prefix cannot exceed the total number of elements available."
